<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basil Lekapenos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 925–<a href="/years/985">985</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Basil the Parakoimomenos, Basil the Nothos, Basileios Lekapenos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basil Lekapenos, called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parakoimomenos">Parakoimomenos</a> or the Nothos (the Bastard), was an illegitimate son of the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-i-lekapenos">Romanos I Lekapenos</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> who served as grand chamberlain and chief minister for most of the period from 947 to 985. He served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a>, helped bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-phokas">Nikephoros II Phokas</a> to the throne in 963 with some 3,000 armed attendants, and ran the fiscal administration under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a>, amassing vast estates in southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. In 985 his great-nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> removed him, confiscated his possessions and annulled his laws, and he died in exile soon after. A lavish patron, he commissioned the enamelled cross reliquary now at Limburg Cathedral.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most slaves served in rich households: the widow Danielis of Patras gave Basil I 3,000 slaves, and the eunuch <b>Basil Lekapenos</b> was said to own 3,000 slaves and retainers.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li></ul></section>
